 you know when you look  between assays from different companies  um  it tends to be a wider or more difficult  problem in terms of  the  the bias that you might introduce by um  switching  um  not only  assays but assay platforms  um and the best way to get it that i  wish you know that's been one of the  biggest  problems in this area  both particularly with ebv  and cmv is that  laboratories a number of years ago  transitioned many laboratories  transitioned from laboratory develop  tests to  fda cleared test  and  there  often are wide differences when you when  you make that um make that leap  and so labs that are still using  laboratory develop tests may have a  harder time  lining up the results between the fda  cleared test and the laboratory  developed tests but the easiest way to  get to that was going to be  by by doing parallel testing  with the different assays for some  period of time and to establish what  that you know difference in results  will be  when the  switch is made and then bring  the clinician users of the essay  into that discussion  to help them interpret you know what the  differences might be  thank you so much dr nolte and i i want  to thank our audience members for these  great great questions coming in we have  time for a few more  this next audience member says kind of  connected to the vl threshold questions  qpcr assays have become more and more  sensitive do you see that affecting  patient management for example would you  continue to treat a cmv positive patient  with viral load results less than lod  yeah  another good question and one that um  [Music]  really does need to be addressed the the  sensitivity of the assays  the analytical sensitivity is quite good  and  may be better  than  certainly some of the laboratory tests  develop tests are deployed and and may  be better than some of the other  results that you would see on other ibd  platforms  um so that that raises the question you  know what would you do if you were  